WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.891
Is time real? What is time? Each moment of time is equally real.

00:00:03.891 --> 00:00:07.680
Past exists. The future exists. When we measure it, what we're doing is getting entangled

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.010
with the particle. We see one version of it, and another version of us is another version.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.853
You're finding yourself in these different universes.

00:00:13.853 --> 00:00:17.147
The whole of physical reality looks a bit like a collection of worlds.

00:00:17.147 --> 00:00:20.120
Future in the past already exists. You're just in a slice.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:25.172
We are using a common sense idea of what a measurement is, but really we ought to have

00:00:25.172 --> 00:00:27.995
a theory of what a measurement is in quantum mechanics.

00:00:27.995 --> 00:00:31.600
This then lead people to naturally think that everything is deterministic.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.782
We are here in a beautiful room in Oxford, and speaking of Oxford,

00:00:37.782 --> 00:00:43.176
my understanding is you worked with David Deutsch, sort of like the father of quantum computing.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:48.000
How is it to work with him? Like, how does the scientist like him get to all these results

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.337
when, you know, there are like thousands of other scientists who don't have all the achievements

00:00:52.337 --> 00:00:54.253
he has had in his life?

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:56.364
I can't tell you how he did what he did.

00:00:56.364 --> 00:01:02.012
All I can say is that it was very nice working with him, and I think the main thing important

00:01:02.012 --> 00:01:05.648
is to have the right ideas about certain topics.

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:11.855
My view of the history of quantum information theory is partly that a bunch of people

00:01:11.855 --> 00:01:16.145
were interested in taking quantum theory seriously, and that includes David,

00:01:16.145 --> 00:01:20.885
but it also includes people like Shilpa Sark and Charlie Bennett,

00:01:20.885 --> 00:01:24.577
who were also interested in kind of a pure view of common theory,

00:01:24.577 --> 00:01:27.600
as I put it, or maybe many worlds view of it.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:35.120
And although it's, I think, it's less explicit with them, it still leads to certain conclusions

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.360
in the foundations of physics and also in cryptography and a bunch of other topics,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.751
where there are ramifications to taking quantum theory as the literal description of

00:01:45.751 --> 00:01:52.080
the world. And one of them is that if you have classical theories,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.640
such as classical cryptographic theories, and you think that classical physics is actually

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:02.768
wrong, well, then you might want to revise what you know about cryptography in light

00:02:02.768 --> 00:02:05.456
of quantum mechanics and stuff like that.

00:02:05.456 --> 00:02:09.910
Yeah, pops up, and I think that is also true of David's work.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:14.000
Yeah, a lot of it is, I guess, the general scientific values,

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.553
things like being curious, things like being critical of the theories that we have.

00:02:18.553 --> 00:02:21.909
Yeah, trying to push them as far as we can, stuff like that.

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:27.431
I think one of the problems he has been encountering is that a lot of the scientists,

00:02:27.431 --> 00:02:32.405
like in quantum mechanics, who are focused, you know, mechanics,

00:02:32.405 --> 00:02:38.300
are not necessarily willing to just relearn everything from scratch through this new,

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.620
like, framework that is like Heisenberg mechanics.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.554
And so, yeah, there's little growth there as a result, and it's basically like one of

00:02:45.554 --> 00:02:47.537
the only ones who is like actively in it.

00:02:47.537 --> 00:02:48.194
So, yeah.

00:02:48.194 --> 00:02:50.821
So the funny thing is that it's actually quite an old framework,

00:02:50.821 --> 00:02:51.321
and people are not willing to—

00:02:51.321 --> 00:02:51.840
Exactly, yeah. It's kind of an old framework, and people are not willing to—

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.649
Exactly. Ironic.

00:02:52.649 --> 00:02:56.693
Yeah, I mean, it was, it was invented by Heisenberg.

00:02:56.693 --> 00:02:59.120
Yes, when quantum theory was invented.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.627
Yeah, almost 100 years ago now. I think 100 years ago, actually.

00:03:03.627 --> 00:03:10.063
And with that, I also feel like there are other reasons people do not take it on board.

00:03:10.063 --> 00:03:16.020
I mean, I agree wholeheartedly with Charles, and we, yeah, are in agree.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.840
So the topic that this is about in particular is something called locality.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.903
And local realism is the view that, roughly speaking, there is a real world,

00:03:27.903 --> 00:03:33.212
and you can divide it into parts, and when you do something to one part,

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:34.348
you can't affect another.

00:03:34.348 --> 00:03:37.900
Yeah, if you want to, there's no spooky action at a distance.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:44.080
Exactly, yeah. So if I, if I have something here, if I have a cup of water or something,

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.485
yeah, and it's spatially separated from another item, then doing something to the water

00:03:48.485 --> 00:03:52.189
should not affect whatever else, yes, there is that I'm considering,

00:03:52.189 --> 00:03:56.720
unless they're interacting directly at a particular point, like at the same location,

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.040
say, or through something mediating the interaction.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.040
Then those are cases in which you can interacting, they can affect each other.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.120
If that's not the case, they shouldn't affect each other.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.920
And quantum theory, it's generally thought that does happen.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.008
So, but I can explain the general setup if you want.

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:20.016
Yeah, let's go.

00:04:20.016 --> 00:04:26.960
Yeah, so, for example, there are cases where two systems are what we call entangled,

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:33.815
and that means the following. You can have a quantum particle with a certain property,

00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:39.821
say we call spin, which is a little bit like having a magnetic moment,

00:04:39.821 --> 00:04:43.675
but it's slightly more subtle, but that's all right.

00:04:43.675 --> 00:04:46.212
So the particle can have spin up and down.

00:04:46.212 --> 00:04:48.508
So it can be found in one of these two states.

00:04:48.508 --> 00:04:54.400
It can also be found in which case some of the time when you look at it has value up,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.440
and some of the time when you look at it has value down, seemingly at random.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.660
And now it can be the case that there's another particle with the same property,

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.780
up and down, which is correlated to that of the other one.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.282
So we have two particles. Each of them separately can have the property of having spin

00:05:12.282 --> 00:05:13.714
up or having spin down.

00:05:13.714 --> 00:05:15.944
So they're kind of like linked in a way.

00:05:15.944 --> 00:05:19.693
Yeah, it seems like. So it seems like they're linked, but that is,

00:05:19.693 --> 00:05:23.701
that already seems to suggest that there could be such a thing as protection.

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:29.424
So the thing which can happen is that when it couldn't, it can be the case that the system,

00:05:29.424 --> 00:05:34.028
so both particles, are in a superposition such that when you measure particle one,

00:05:34.028 --> 00:05:37.956
let's call it particle one, let's go the other one particle two,

00:05:37.956 --> 00:05:42.744
if you measure particle one having spin up, then the other particle also has spin up.

00:05:42.744 --> 00:05:46.712
And if you measure particle one with spin down, the other particle also has spin down,

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:50.896
and vice versa. So if you measure the second particle and spin up,

00:05:50.896 --> 00:05:56.946
the first particle has been up, etc. And that seems very bizarre because the outcomes

00:05:56.946 --> 00:06:02.772
appear to be random. So if you perform a measurement here, you,

00:06:02.772 --> 00:06:07.120
so I have a particle, you have the other particle, the entangled pair.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.604
We go very far away from each other, and then we perform measurements.

00:06:13.604 --> 00:06:14.104
Yeah.

00:06:14.104 --> 00:06:20.880
Now I'm, I measure my particle, I see it up, and I, now it seems like I instantly know

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.312
that you also have up, because that is what entanglement tells us.

00:06:25.312 --> 00:06:28.640
So it's as if there was faster than light communication in a way.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.421
Exactly, yeah. It seems like. So it's not communication because I can't message anything

00:06:32.421 --> 00:06:32.977
to you.

00:06:32.977 --> 00:06:33.811
Yes.

00:06:33.811 --> 00:06:37.902
But there does seem to be some kind of influence which is superluminal.

00:06:37.902 --> 00:06:43.890
Yeah, and that's very bizarre. I mean, one of the reasons it's bizarre is that means,

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:48.048
so in, it seems to be instantaneous description that we have.

00:06:48.048 --> 00:06:51.040
That means that in certain frames, it's going backwards in time.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:56.375
So we know that in special relativity, simultaneity is not absolute.

00:06:56.375 --> 00:07:01.269
If something is instantaneous in one frame, then there's another frame which the effect

00:07:01.269 --> 00:07:05.183
propagates backwards in time, which doesn't seem physical at all.

00:07:05.183 --> 00:07:05.691
Yes.

00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:12.597
So this is an issue. And now there is actually, in many worlds,

00:07:12.597 --> 00:07:19.743
actually offers quite a nice solution to this, which we can get into as well.

00:07:19.743 --> 00:07:24.065
But maybe, I don't know, maybe we should first talk about what many worlds is in particular.

00:07:24.065 --> 00:07:28.667
But so this is the, this is one of the issues, yeah, that I and Charles and other people

00:07:28.667 --> 00:07:31.120
like, yeah, and David also have worked on.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.570
We're in the minority in holding the opinion that actual spooky action at a distance,

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.813
so this thing that we just described, is not normal.

00:07:38.813 --> 00:07:41.642
Yeah, not something that actually happens.

00:07:41.642 --> 00:07:47.271
Yeah, I think, I feel like even most people do not really see it as an issue anymore.

00:07:47.271 --> 00:07:49.960
So most physicists are just accepted and just buying the bulletin and say,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.616
well, that is just what con theory tells us.

00:07:51.616 --> 00:07:52.536
It is what it is.

00:07:52.536 --> 00:07:53.036
Yeah.

00:07:53.036 --> 00:07:58.632
So I think that's part of the reason why Charles and other than I also have experience

00:07:58.632 --> 00:08:01.068
like slightly harder. Paul also, by the way.

00:08:01.068 --> 00:08:05.385
Paul, yeah, I forgot to mention him, but he had some very important work.

00:08:05.385 --> 00:08:05.885
Yes.

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:11.655
And I feel like that's why we have a somewhat hard time arguing with people that actually

00:08:11.655 --> 00:08:14.282
we should, this is an issue and we should find a solution.

00:08:14.282 --> 00:08:14.782
Yes.

00:08:14.782 --> 00:08:16.943
And then we think we have a solution. So it's kind of twofold.

00:08:16.943 --> 00:08:19.920
Whereas usually you can argue with people, well, there is a problem and this is what

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.840
we think the solution is. And here we have to say, well, it's not just that we have a solution.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.116
We have, first of all, we have to convince you that there is an issue here.

00:08:27.116 --> 00:08:31.520
Yes. Yeah, people are just, are not willing to accept it in the first place.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.764
Yeah, I think that, as I said, I think they bite the bullet,

00:08:33.764 --> 00:08:37.280
and they've just accepted the world. That's what one of these tells us about the world

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.045
is very counterintuitive. Yeah, I mean, so be it.

00:08:40.045 --> 00:08:42.610
Yeah, and, but I think that's not quite satisfying.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:46.975
Before we get into many worlds, my understanding is, so you were previously researcher

00:08:46.975 --> 00:08:51.760
first at Oxford, then at the University of Montreal, which is where we met.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:52.260
Yes.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:54.140
Now your researcher for Conjecture Institute.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.457
What does that entail?

00:08:55.457 --> 00:08:59.600
It basically means I do the same kinds of things, but I work for Conjecture Institute.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.960
So I'm still writing papers. I have a couple of papers in the pipeline.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:03.460
Nice.

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:09.760
And my day-to-day life looks very similar, except I now work for a Conjecture Institute,

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.480
which is very exciting.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.060
And what is Conjecture Institute?

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:18.907
So Conjecture Institute is a private foundation which sponsors people,

00:09:18.907 --> 00:09:20.720
including you and me.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:21.764
Yeah, you're another.

00:09:21.764 --> 00:09:25.244
Yeah, I mean, at least it sponsors me and you.

00:09:25.244 --> 00:09:33.235
I know that you are also a fellow. They try to fund neglected topics in physics and other

00:09:33.235 --> 00:09:39.760
areas of research and thought. I'm mainly there for the physics,

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.088
and so there's a bunch of topics I care about that they care about as well,

00:09:43.088 --> 00:09:48.164
such as many worlds, such as locality, these kinds of things.

00:09:48.164 --> 00:09:55.440
And it's a very exciting next step in my research career.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.474
So it's fun. I enjoy it.

00:09:57.474 --> 00:09:59.170
Let's get into many worlds.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:00.187
So, cool, yeah.

00:10:00.187 --> 00:10:04.373
What is many worlds? Like, many worlds, like this is actually in jail.

00:10:04.373 --> 00:10:06.187
Like, what is even this war like?

00:10:06.187 --> 00:10:30.800
Yeah, this is a weird thing. Yeah, it's a very interesting thing to think about.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.135
Some of the assumptions which are not problematic, which are widely accepted,

00:10:34.135 --> 00:10:38.600
and we think should just be part of the theories it's properly understood.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:43.860
Things like what we just mentioned, systems can be in superposition of different states,

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.460
they can be entangled with other systems.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:50.767
There's a few others, like the way they evolve should be of a particular type,

00:10:50.767 --> 00:10:54.680
should be what is prescribed by the Schrodinger equation, for instance.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:58.960
And so those are all fine, those are all commonly accepted.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:05.560
And then there is one axiom, one assumption, which is both ill-formulated and problematic,

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:12.700
namely the measurement postulate, which basically says that when you look at a system,

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.500
which is in a superposition-- - Let's clarify, what is a system?

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:21.076
- Well, it could really be anything. I mean, we can make it more,

00:11:21.076 --> 00:11:23.560
so it-- - It could be like a particle in a box, for example.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:26.868
- It could be atoms, yes, a particle in a box, could be photons,

00:11:26.868 --> 00:11:27.780
could be things like that.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:33.620
So usually, these systems are small, so we're considering small stuff.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.880
- Yeah, quantum, yeah, microscopic, yeah.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:43.524
- Yeah, so, although I always think that is, the theory is usually presented as being

00:11:43.524 --> 00:11:45.240
about small things and it's actually not.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:50.109
I used to be into solid state physics, which is physics that tries to describe,

00:11:50.109 --> 00:11:53.020
for example, how metals work and how-- - Yeah, materials, yeah.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:58.740
- Yeah, exactly, how insulators, a whole range of materials,

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:02.940
function and behave and what their properties are.

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.520
And that whole area of physics is completely dominated by quantum mechanics.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:11.122
Classical physics doesn't really play much of a role in that study,

00:12:11.122 --> 00:12:12.960
and those are macroscopic materials.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.200
Like, we couldn't have stable objects if it wasn't for quantum mechanics.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:21.380
We know from classical physics that atoms, classical atoms are not stable.

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:24.620
So if atoms aren't stable and things are supposed to be made out of atoms,

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:28.520
they'll think you can't have a classical description of a table consisting of atoms.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:34.780
That is, in any way, stable. They should collapse immediately into like the nucleus or something.

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:39.833
To clarify, we mean specifically in this case, things like maybe an atom,

00:12:39.833 --> 00:12:42.780
maybe electrons, maybe photons. It could be in a box.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:47.540
It could be experimenting with them in some laboratory setting.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:53.800
- Yeah. - And they can have this property that they are in a superposition.

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:58.300
So maybe the electron is in a box somewhere and it's in two places at once.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:04.480
In the box. So it's like roughly either here or there in the box.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:09.520
And now the measurement posture tells us that if we were to look at the box,

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:15.460
you would never see two electrons because it's just one electron.

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:21.388
You only see one of them. - Yes. - And you see one of them with some probability determined

00:13:21.388 --> 00:13:24.240
by the exact configuration the system's in.

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:28.080
It could be 50/50. It's always a simplifying assumption.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.860
It could also not be 50/50. But again, it's just for simplicity.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:32.700
Sounds fair enough.

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:34.660
Sounds like, okay, well, that could be an assumption.

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:39.000
And the first thing that I find annoying is that we don't really tell people what is

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:45.220
this measurement. A measurement is a thing which we know from common sense what that's

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:48.800
supposed to be. It's like, if I look at a thing in a box, that's a measurement.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:52.220
- Yeah. It's not a preservation. Yeah. - Yeah.

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:54.700
It's not something that the theory tells us.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:14:02.640
- Yeah. - That is to say, we are using a common sense idea of what a measurement is.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:06.900
But really, we ought to have a theory of what a measurement is in quantum mechanics.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:11.620
And that's not the case. So we have kind of an undefined term within the axiomatic system

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.040
that is quantum mechanics. - Yeah. Like, for example, it's not defined.

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.720
Is it when it reaches the eyes?

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.400
Or is it in the brain? Does it work if I use a camera?

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:26.360
It's also a form of observation. - Yes. - Yeah, that's very unclear.

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:30.320
Yeah. - Yeah, exactly. Yeah. So there's a bunch of things which it doesn't specify.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:36.160
- Yeah. - And so that should ring some alarm bells.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:43.880
And the other thing that's kind of strange about it is that this is a kind of dynamic.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:46.240
So the system, so this is called collapse.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:49.580
So we measure a system, and it is in a superposition.

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:53.800
It's set to collapse into one of these two states, or it could be multiple states,

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.400
but it collapses to a singular state.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:01.080
And... That appears to be irreversible. - Yeah.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:05.718
- And that is not compatible with, for example, the kind of evolution that the Schrodinger

00:15:05.718 --> 00:15:08.920
picture, or the Schrodinger equation prescribes.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:13.280
So there's already a bunch of issues, even just in trying to say,

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:17.500
trying to formalize what the axiom is supposed to say.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:24.209
The many worlds interpretation or explanation says instead that collapse doesn't happen

00:15:24.209 --> 00:15:30.460
and that we can derive all the results that we typically get out through this axiom,

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:35.780
just from the typical kind of evolution that the Schrodinger equation prescribes.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.660
So we're essentially able to explain more with less.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:42.040
We have fewer assumptions, but we can explain the same things.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:45.460
And also we have a richer picture of what's actually going on in the world.

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:52.480
And what that actually entails in, for example, the setting we were just talking about,

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.980
is that when there's these two possibilities, so the electron...

00:15:56.980 --> 00:16:01.660
The electron is in two places at once. Then when we measure it,

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:06.560
what we're doing is getting entangled with the particle so that we see one version of it.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.880
And another version of us sees another version.

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:14.840
So version one of me sees electrons here, another version of me sees electrons over there.

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:18.800
And they don't interact with each other generally.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:21.680
So it appears as if the other version has disappeared from view.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:28.000
But that's all something which comes out of the Schrodinger equation essentially.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.080
- What collapse says is, so we have this particle in the box.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:34.900
It's maybe in like two states, like it's in a superposition of two states.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:37.880
So it's kind of like in this mix of states, like it's unclear which is which,

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:41.220
but there's like a, you know, as if you were to like, you know,

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:45.880
mix several liquids, for example, like it's sort of like in a mix of both states.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:53.040
And when we, in the collapse approach, like the traditional way of thinking about it,

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:59.480
when we look at it, the particle will collapse into either of those states.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:04.020
So states like A and B, for example, and then, and that's why we see only A or B,

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:06.420
even throwing the equations, you would see A and B.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:12.269
- Yes. - And then what many worlds says is when I, when I look at the particle,

00:17:12.269 --> 00:17:15.920
I get entangled with it. So I get, you know, connected.

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:24.660
And that leads me to see in one branch, we're getting through the branches,

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.995
the states, the particle in state A, and in the other branch,

00:17:27.995 --> 00:17:32.220
in particle B, and many worlds says both of those are equally real.

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:33.880
Is that correct? - Yeah, they're equally real.

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:39.607
And so one of the benefits of this is that we don't have to assume different kinds of

00:17:39.607 --> 00:17:42.060
dynamics when we're performing a measurement.

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:43.800
We also don't have to assume what a measurement is.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:49.120
We can derive a model of what a measurement is from the theory itself,

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:56.100
so that we don't have these undefined words that we're using in setting up the theory.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:01.853
And it also kind of makes sense of what it even means for the particle to be in a superposition

00:18:01.853 --> 00:18:05.780
in the first place, because in the conventional view, it's kind of unclear.

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:08.820
It's not clear what it means for the particle to be in superposition.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:12.240
It just means it's either here or there kind of potentially.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.580
- Yes, and there's a probability and it's, yeah.

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:17.360
- Yeah, so it's vague.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:22.107
Whereas in many worlds too, it just means, well, there is, the particle really is in

00:18:22.107 --> 00:18:27.847
two places at once and it's behaving exactly as if it is actually here and actually there

00:18:27.847 --> 00:18:34.420
at the same time. And that is true for things which get entangled with it as well.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:41.180
So it gives a more explanatory framework for understanding how quantum theory actually works.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:46.700
So there's both the formal benefits and then at the level of the explanation,

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:52.420
I feel it's much more intuitive. So those would be some of the reasons why I like theory.

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:59.320
- We have those two branches now. And what we're seeing is that those are equally real.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.120
What does this actually imply? Like, where are we getting to?

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:06.342
So there are many worlds then? - Yes. - Kind of like bringing it up,

00:19:06.342 --> 00:19:09.340
but. - Yeah, it's in the name. You've got the name, right?

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:17.080
Yes, so there are different worlds in which, for example, as I've said,

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:20.080
it could be small things which initiate the branching.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:25.440
So I see particle being here instead of there.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:29.920
And consequently, that kind of quantum effect gets amplified.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:34.340
And now I have a record of where in my mind, I have a record of where the particle was,

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:40.180
as does the environment from then on. It could be that this information is somehow leaked

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:44.200
to the environment. So maybe to make it a little bit artificial,

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:52.100
but let's say that I have decided if I see the particle in state of being here,

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:54.180
then I'm gonna cook some dinner.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:57.280
And if I see the particle here, well, then I'm gonna read a book.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.797
And then after a little while, you'll see that the kitchen is messy in one branch and

00:20:00.797 --> 00:20:07.880
it's not in the other. And so these small differences in the state of an initial,

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:13.426
like just a particle, just a microscopic particle, within that case has been amplified

00:20:13.426 --> 00:20:17.060
up to cause a mess in the kitchen, in one branch and not the other.

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:18.880
That kind of thing is constantly going on.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:25.000
So there's constantly things are in superposition, like through natural processes.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.460
And then they happen to get measured, where measured really in this case,

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:32.840
could mean just information about where the particle is, gets encoded in the environment,

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:35.860
gets encoded in me, in the mind, or in other things.

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:46.520
And from then on, these kind of differentiating effects percolate throughout the environment.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:51.340
So there's more and more records are in the environment of where the particle was.

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:54.780
And that means that more and more of the world is different,

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.680
one branch than it was in the other one.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:21:00.500
So you get these branches, which are initially different only in small ways,

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:02.560
and then they become different in larger ways.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:07.080
And because of that, it's harder and harder for them to reinterfere,

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:11.160
to influence each other again. - Yeah, so it is a good way to think about it,

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:14.360
that it's sort of like a bubble growing of entanglements.

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:17.400
And so let's say, you have the particle in the box.

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:20.620
So I'm the observer in the laboratory. I'm looking at the particle.

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:24.560
I get entangled with it because the information reaches my eyes and my brain.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:31.282
And then just all of me. And then maybe I will open the door and the information will

00:21:31.282 --> 00:21:37.180
reach the neighborhood around it, and then eventually the whole world.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:41.088
And so, but there is a separate version of me that saw state B,

00:21:41.088 --> 00:21:45.260
like the particle in state B, and that for which the same happens.

00:21:45.260 --> 00:21:48.400
- Yes. - And so eventually that's the process we call decoherence,

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:52.120
where eventually we're getting split.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:57.100
And those branches are becoming more and more separate universes,

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:59.960
which is, you know, what we'll call multiverse sometimes.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:05.660
- Yeah. Yeah, so the whole of physical reality looks a bit like a collection of worlds

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:08.160
that we know from classical physics. - Yes.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:14.531
- They have other properties as well. So for example, one of the things we haven't mentioned

00:22:14.531 --> 00:22:19.900
yet is that particles and other quantum systems can interfere.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:22.920
And that's one of the primary ways that we know about quantum mechanics.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:27.687
- Yes. Yes. - Interference effects happen in, for example, the double slit,

00:22:27.687 --> 00:22:34.220
which is this famous set up where you have two slits with the backdrop being a wall or

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:37.889
some detector, and you send a particle through one at a time,

00:22:37.889 --> 00:22:38.960
could be photon.

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:44.631
And then when you detect where the photon is on the screen, after having repeated this

00:22:44.631 --> 00:22:48.340
experiment in series, so you send one through and two at a time,

00:22:48.340 --> 00:22:51.660
and you detect it, and then you send another one through, and then you detect it again,

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:55.272
then you find that there's an interference pattern, which wouldn't be there if you closed

00:22:55.272 --> 00:22:59.240
either of the two slits. - But what do you mean with an interference pattern?

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:04.582
- It means that there are places on the screen where many photons arrive and places where

00:23:04.582 --> 00:23:07.920
barely any or none arrive. And they kind of, they come in fringes.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:12.320
So it's like, you know, a lot, less, a lot.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:16.100
And then again, much fewer or none, a lot.

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:23.680
And that keeps going on. And that pattern is called an interference pattern because the...

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:28.160
What's happening is that, for example, in the case of the photon,

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:32.700
if you send it through the two slits, it actually goes through both.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.340
- Yes. - And we'll get to why that's the case.

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:42.653
It goes through both. And then there's an instance of the photon going through the left

00:23:42.653 --> 00:23:45.840
one and one through the right one, which then end up affecting each other.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:51.300
So they end up interfering, which just means they affect each other just as they might.

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:56.040
It's a little bit similar to a classical way of theory where if you have waves,

00:23:56.040 --> 00:24:00.440
one of the things that can happen is they can interact and then amplify each other.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:05.780
Or if you have a trough and a peak, then they could cancel.

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:07.000
And that's what's going on at the screen.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:11.796
So some versions of the photon are amplifying each other and some versions are interacting

00:24:11.796 --> 00:24:16.100
to cancel each other. And as I said, if you do that many times,

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:20.540
each time you're sending a single photon through, then you get this interference pattern.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:23.240
- Yes. - And the kind of the bizarre thing is if you close up,

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:26.420
close either of these two slits, then it disappears.

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:34.856
So that indicates that something must be going through the other slit to interact with

00:24:34.856 --> 00:24:40.720
the thing that goes through the one that isn't closed, so to give the pattern.

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:45.033
Otherwise you would still see the pattern if only one slit were open,

00:24:45.033 --> 00:24:48.680
which we don't. Again, the many worlds explanation is there's two versions of the photon.

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:53.598
There's only ever one photon, but the photon can consist of multiple instances or versions

00:24:53.598 --> 00:24:57.240
or however you want to call it. And they can affect each other.

00:24:57.240 --> 00:25:02.442
They can interfere. So we know from seeing the pattern that the photon was in two places

00:25:02.442 --> 00:25:09.052
at once. It took two paths at once. And that is generally, that's one of the main ways

00:25:09.052 --> 00:25:15.306
that we know about quantum effects is that we see interference happening in what are

00:25:15.306 --> 00:25:18.720
called generally interference experiments, of which this is an instance.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:25.598
- This effectively means that there are billions if not an infinity of versions of you

00:25:25.598 --> 00:25:29.160
and I in these different worlds.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:32.380
Where are these worlds? Like, wait, are they somewhere?

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:35.180
Like, can I go there? Like, can I visit?

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:40.100
- So, firstly, there's not gonna be infinitely many.

00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:44.515
There are reasons to believe that there is a bound on how many different universes there

00:25:44.515 --> 00:25:50.220
can be. - Interesting. - That comes from, it's the so-called Bekenstein bound,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:56.131
and it gives an argument for why in any finite region there is an upper bound to the

00:25:56.131 --> 00:26:02.880
amount of universes that can be there. - Is that due to the number of interactions possible?

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:06.360
- It is due to the amount of information you can store in a system.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:12.580
So this is maybe a bit technical, but the way to think about it is the different universes,

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:16.740
it should be the case that they do not interfere with each other.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:22.000
They could in principle, but we generally want them to evolve independently.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:26.520
For that to be the case, they have to be orthogonal, which is a technical term which

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:34.453
I don't think I can explain. But we generally want the universes to be orthogonal to

00:26:34.453 --> 00:26:37.080
one another. - Separate in a way. - Yes.

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:40.200
- Yes, very distinct. - Yes, yeah, they don't have overlap.

00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:47.448
And the number of orthogonal states that you can have in a finite region of space is

00:26:47.448 --> 00:26:51.780
determined by the maximum entropy in that region, and the maximum entropy is bounded.

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:57.026
- Yes, so there's a limit capacity of how much you can store,

00:26:57.026 --> 00:26:59.020
essentially. - Yeah, exactly, yeah.

00:26:59.020 --> 00:27:02.760
So, Bekenstein's bound gives an upper bound, and that is a little bit technical.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:07.933
I think that's the most technical thing we've said so far, but the main idea is there

00:27:07.933 --> 00:27:15.440
is some bound which determines things like how much information you can store in a system,

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:16.980
and also how many universes there can be.

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:21.060
- Where are these universes? - An enlarged space that we call Hilbert space.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:27.567
- Yeah. - And in some sense, the universe is, it kind of depends on our views of space

00:27:27.567 --> 00:27:32.900
and time, but in some sense, they are here in space.

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:36.380
- Yes. - But they are in different points in Hilbert space.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:39.900
So Hilbert space is this larger space in which quantum states live.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:45.360
- But it's a mathematical space, right? Like, we're not talking about like,

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.742
necessarily like physical space, like going to the moon or something,

00:27:48.742 --> 00:27:53.380
yeah. - Yeah, no, it's a mathematical space, and it's not the same thing as string theory

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:56.580
having multiple dimensions or anything. - Yes.

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:00.928
- Everyone who does quantum physics is in agreement that there is this thing called Hilbert

00:28:00.928 --> 00:28:03.880
space in which the states of a quantum system live.

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:06.680
I think the best answer I can give is they live in Hilbert space.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:10.575
Another way of saying it is that they exist in some sense, they are just as real as we

00:28:10.575 --> 00:28:17.620
are, because we also live in Hilbert space, but they're just not where we are,

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:21.120
otherwise we would see them. - So there's no way to interact with them?

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:24.780
Like once they're like orthogonal, as you said, once they're like distinct,

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:29.658
like once the branch is distinct, there's no way to interact with them,

00:28:29.658 --> 00:28:33.840
right? - So maybe we should say that we have all these different terms and they basically

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:38.300
mean the same thing. So we have the terms branches and universes and things like that.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:39.640
- Universes, worlds, it's all the same thing.

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:43.810
- Yeah, it's all basically just parts of a quantum system that don't really interact

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:47.740
with other parts anymore and evolve as if the other parts aren't there.

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:53.757
And sometimes also we have the connotation that we mean, they are obeying semi-classical

00:28:53.757 --> 00:28:55.900
operations of motion.

00:28:55.900 --> 00:29:01.112
They look like they're classical systems effectively, is what we might also mean,

00:29:01.112 --> 00:29:06.244
but at any rate, so just to clarify some terminology, it's always the case that they

00:29:06.244 --> 00:29:08.140
can interact in principle, which is very hard.

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:11.743
- It's increasingly unlikely. - Yeah, it's increasingly difficult essentially,

00:29:11.743 --> 00:29:20.680
yeah. Like if, for example, in the photon experiment, you're reinterfering a single photon

00:29:20.680 --> 00:29:28.720
and that's quite a manageable task. It's much more difficult to make a couple of photons

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:32.100
interfere with one another. So they would interfere.

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:34.040
Well, maybe that's not the best example.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:38.000
So let's say that the photon becomes entangled with the environment somehow.

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:43.126
Then it's not just the photon that we need to reinterfere, we also need to reinterfere

00:29:43.126 --> 00:29:44.880
the thing that the photon is now entangled with.

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:48.960
- Yes, so we need to undo essentially our changes in a way.

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:53.640
- Yeah, and the reason for that is, roughly, that for interference to happen,

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:55.700
the photon really should have taken both paths.

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:59.038
So one instance of the photon should take one path and the other one should take the

00:29:59.038 --> 00:30:04.460
other one. When the photon becomes entangled with the environment,

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:07.460
it essentially even traces of where it was.

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:14.202
And that means that there's now a definite answer in that world where the photon was

00:30:14.202 --> 00:30:19.460
actually at, which is information that you can't have if the photon literally took both paths.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:23.360
You can't say, well, I know that the photon took this one, but also it was in both.

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:30.220
- Yes. - That's logically incoherent. So instead, the photon shouldn't have any information.

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:33.040
I think it's sometimes called which-where information.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:37.580
That means that either you erase that information before the interference happens,

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:43.215
or the thing that was entangled with the photon also interferes with the other version

00:30:43.215 --> 00:30:45.680
of itself so that information gets erased.

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:47.300
But somehow you need to get rid of that.

00:30:47.300 --> 00:30:51.993
And it's hard because of course the photon and other parts are constantly leaving ever

00:30:51.993 --> 00:30:55.220
more traces in their environment of where they were.

00:30:55.220 --> 00:31:00.486
So that means you have like an exponentially cascading number of things to undo before

00:31:00.486 --> 00:31:06.020
you can re-interfere the thing again. - One of the, I guess one of the criticisms,

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:09.220
I guess you could say, or like the common objections, let's say,

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:15.493
of many worlds is this idea of energy. Because, well, if you're telling me that we're

00:31:15.493 --> 00:31:20.400
now going to have all these parallel, quote unquote, universes,

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:24.000
where does the energy come from?

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:26.040
So we're just like duplicating each time?

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:30.580
Like, how is this actually possible? - Yeah, so the funny thing is that intuitively that

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:32.880
seems very strange. And actually, if you look at the mathematics,

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:39.535
it's much simpler. - Yes. - So in the case of it, so people would expect that somehow

00:31:39.535 --> 00:31:42.480
many worlds violates energy conservation.

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:49.580
And in fact, I think generally is a measurement which does, because in...

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:57.740
In the many worlds picture, you only ever have unitary evolution and that preserves the

00:31:57.740 --> 00:32:01.080
energy of a system. - What is unitary evolution?

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:05.540
- So we only ever have things like Schrodinger type evolution.

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:09.860
- So like linear, clean line. - Yeah, reversible, all of the good stuff.

00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:15.960
- Yeah. - We like this, we like this. - It's simple, it's beautiful.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:19.360
- Yes, and that preserves the energy of a system.

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:25.480
- Yes. - And measurement does not have that, and so you're not guaranteed to conserve

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:28.960
the energy. - Yes. - Anyway, so there's one note.

00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:35.492
The way I view it, and I think lots of people who are into many worlds view as well,

00:32:35.492 --> 00:32:39.580
is that this view of splitting the universe is not right.

00:32:39.580 --> 00:32:42.260
So it's not like you are splitting the universe.

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:47.393
It's more like every system already is a collection of instances,

00:32:47.393 --> 00:32:54.440
all of which are completely identical initially, as kind of like a slap of the multiverse.

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:57.980
And then they, some of them become distinct.

00:32:57.980 --> 00:33:02.200
So like maybe first it's this many, and then some of them, you know.

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:07.640
- So we're constantly like slicing the same block of energy in a way.

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:15.240
- Yes. - And so, yeah, so it remains constant across all of those branches,

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:18.920
but the branches themselves may have a smaller slice.

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:20.340
- Yeah, exactly, yeah.

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.760
So it's not like you're making mass. - Yes.

00:33:22.760 --> 00:33:25.369
- It's not like, oh, you, something happens, something entangled,

00:33:25.369 --> 00:33:29.620
something entangles with another thing, and now there's more mass because there's more universes.

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:34.395
It's more like there was already a certain amount of them, which we can have a general

00:33:34.395 --> 00:33:37.680
measure of. - They were just identical, and now we're splitting them.

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:40.660
- Exactly, now they're, yeah. So splitting, or they're branching.

00:33:40.660 --> 00:33:45.738
- Yeah, slicing, yeah. - All of that just means some portion of them now do one thing

00:33:45.738 --> 00:33:48.040
and some portion are different and do another.

00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:52.260
- Yeah. - So we're not creating any mass or energy in the process.

00:33:52.260 --> 00:33:55.580
In fact, that is exactly what's conserved.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.420
- A lot of your research is about time.

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:01.840
Is time real? Does time actually exist? What is time?

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:07.760
- So some ways of viewing time or some notions of time don't exist and others do.

00:34:07.760 --> 00:34:12.020
And so I always like to say time doesn't exist.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:16.766
I mean, it's a phrase that Julian Barber and others have used and he wrote a very famous

00:34:16.766 --> 00:34:19.480
book, which I highly recommend called The Convention of Time.

00:34:19.480 --> 00:34:26.240
And the argument in that book is that our general conception of how time works is unnecessary.

00:34:26.240 --> 00:34:33.171
And to explain what that means, the convention of view is something like there is this

00:34:33.171 --> 00:34:37.460
parameter, this number which represents time.

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:43.060
Maybe if you're thinking very classically, then it's the same everywhere in the universe.

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:44.920
Of course, that can't be true because of special relativity,

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:48.260
but never mind that. And it's kind of like God's clock.

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:52.060
Like God has a clock. - It's like his wristwatch and like, yeah.

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:55.700
- And we cannot see it, but we're invoking it because things are changing.

00:34:55.700 --> 00:34:58.860
So things are changing and that means that it's a different time now.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:02.115
The actual things that we use to measure time, like your watch,

00:35:02.115 --> 00:35:06.760
for example, and other things, like the solar system is a thing,

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:12.410
which in some sense is a clock. And the different positions of the planets in the solar

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:16.160
system tell us what time of year it is.

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:18.040
Maybe the seasons are different, things like that.

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:22.000
Those are things internal to the universe which tell us what time is.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:26.560
Whereas the kind of Newtonian view of time is slightly different.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:30.100
It's that, as I said, it's more ethereal.

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:34.440
There is some notion of time. - Across everything.

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:38.200
- Across everything. It's not something that you can necessarily see,

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:40.100
but it's there. - And it's fixed everywhere.

00:35:40.100 --> 00:35:43.980
- It's fixed everywhere, yeah. - It's the same clock for wherever you go.

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:47.820
- Yes. Again, that aspect of it was already no longer tenable,

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:51.860
with Einstein. - Yes, relatively, yeah. - But in some sense,

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:54.780
we don't need to concern ourselves with that so much.

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:57.760
The main thing that's weird is just, it's not something that's observable within the universe.

00:35:57.760 --> 00:36:00.660
We're just saying that there is something that's changing.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:03.560
So this unobservable property of the universe is changing.

00:36:03.560 --> 00:36:07.620
And because that thing is changing, things around us are changing too.

00:36:07.620 --> 00:36:10.555
So people are getting older, but that's because time's passed,

00:36:10.555 --> 00:36:17.166
things like that. And as I already kind of alluded to, the thing that we actually use

00:36:17.166 --> 00:36:22.344
both in everyday life and in science, and I think also just nature more generally uses

00:36:22.344 --> 00:36:27.180
it in some sense, is time that is like internal to the universe.

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:32.180
So clocks are a way of seeing that some change has happened.

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:37.280
So there's some kind of periodic process in a clock and you're counting how often that

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:43.160
process has happened. And that tells you, okay, so the clock says X numbers of periods

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:48.220
have elapsed. I know now that other systems have changed as a result as well,

00:36:48.220 --> 00:36:51.700
because they're constantly undergoing their own physical processes.

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:58.060
Like as your clock is ticking, your wristwatch, the earth is also moving around the sun.

00:36:58.060 --> 00:37:02.780
And we know that a certain number of ticks on your wristwatch corresponds to a certain

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:06.880
distance traveled through space by the earth, things like that.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:14.447
And it turns out even classical physics, as Julian showed, that you can replace this

00:37:14.447 --> 00:37:19.140
ethereal notion of time, this kind of like, okay, the backdrop against which things happen,

00:37:19.140 --> 00:37:21.980
which is itself, this is not really clearly physical.

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:27.300
You can replace that notion of time, which is time as it exists internal to the universe.

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:29.340
Julian's way of doing it is very pretty.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:35.960
What he does is he says, you assume you just have celestial objects and they are obeying

00:37:35.960 --> 00:37:41.410
Newton's equations of motion. So the kind of gravity making them interact and things

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:49.040
like that. And then adding a simple assumption that the energy of the system is equal

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:51.940
to zero. So it's conserved and it's equal to zero.

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:56.880
It turns out that their positions at any particular time are unique.

00:37:56.880 --> 00:38:00.680
So you can kind of use their positions. - To figure out what the time is.

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:04.280
- There exist counterparts of this in quantum mechanics, which are very nice.

00:38:04.280 --> 00:38:07.940
And I work on the quantum counterparts in particular.

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:13.700
So I think both in Julian's view and in my view, as you rephrase that,

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:16.720
I think both in Julian's model, although not on Julian's view,

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:23.564
you get a kind of many-worlds picture again, because to really get rid of time is not

00:38:23.564 --> 00:38:29.840
quite enough to say that time's internal, because you also, if you still have to say,

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:34.680
well, these different configurations are somehow coming about and ceasing to be,

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:40.167
then you need this external number again, which is telling you what the state of the

00:38:40.167 --> 00:38:46.500
system is. So instead, to really get rid of time, one thing you can say is well,

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:52.540
all these configurations of the celestial objects, say, exist together.

00:38:52.540 --> 00:39:01.260
They all exist. And the passage of time is just the change of these celestial objects

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:08.000
relative to you. But there is no external time to take into account.

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:12.660
So they already exist. The future and the past already exist.

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:18.720
You're just in a slice and you can see what time it is, because the configurations are unique.

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:24.960
- The way we can measure time is by observing the change in the world.

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:33.847
- Yes. - And there's no fixed time. And so each of our internal clocks in a way,

00:39:33.847 --> 00:39:35.900
like-- - Well, so that doesn't quite follow.

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:40.840
So again, that becomes more dependent on what specific models that you're talking about.

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:46.060
So in Julian's model, time is fixed everywhere, just by the position of all the celestial objects.

00:39:46.060 --> 00:39:50.740
- Yes. - So in that sense, again, it's the same everywhere.

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:54.500
- Yes. - And, but the main thing about it is that it's internal.

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:58.700
It's like you have this internal label of what the time is, because you can look at the

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:00.520
celestial objects and say, okay, well, these are their positions.

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:05.820
And you can kind of, you can glue them back together, knowing the laws of physics,

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:09.860
you know how to line them up, the sequence.

00:40:09.860 --> 00:40:19.590
And it's that understanding that allows you to replace this unphysical idea of time with

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:24.220
internal labels of what time it is inside the universe.

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:31.280
So it's kind of like, we want to get rid of this weird background structure that we can't observe.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:35.540
Like the theory already tells us, we can't observe this ethereal notion of time,

00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:37.580
this absolute time, as Newton called it.

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:39.946
So instead, we should just look at the things that we can observe,

00:40:39.946 --> 00:40:43.920
like for example, your clock or the positions of celestial bodies.

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:49.020
That is both much more based in observation, and much more satisfying theoretically.

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:53.340
And it leads again to something like a many worlds view.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:59.390
So in particular, we talk mainly about Julian's classical setup,

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:05.200
which I like a lot. And there is a counterpart to this in quantum mechanics,

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:07.600
where it's literally many worlds.

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:18.776
So you assume that you're in the universe, you have the description of the universe or

00:41:18.776 --> 00:41:24.620
the multiverse. And you say, well, it's completely stationary.

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:30.283
We want to get rid of this idea that things are changing relative to the external time

00:41:30.283 --> 00:41:32.280
parameter, relative to the absolute time.

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.560
So let's just assume everything is stationary.

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:38.340
And now let's carve the world up into something we're going to call a clock and the rest

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:45.180
of the universe. And they're not interacting, it's an important assumption.

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:50.840
And then what you say is, well, these two systems are entangled with one another.

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:57.585
So that, for example, when the clock says it's 3:00 PM, then the rest of the universe

00:41:57.585 --> 00:41:59.940
is doing a particular thing.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:05.180
And then when the clock says it's 3:05, then the rest of the universe looks slightly different.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:11.480
And when it's 3:10, then the rest of the universe looks slightly different again.

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:16.340
In such a way that if you keep conditioning on the clock, the rest of the universe evolves

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:18.180
according to the laws of motion that we know.

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:24.000
- Yes. - And then again, you have time coming from a time.

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:28.580
So you have a notion of time that comes from a timeless description in that case.

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:32.518
And it's a many worlds view because the different universes are now just the different

00:42:32.518 --> 00:42:37.390
moments in time and they all exist together in a kind of block in what are sometimes

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:40.420
called the block universe. - There's a clock.

00:42:40.420 --> 00:42:42.080
- Yeah, so you assume that there is a clock.

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:44.740
The model assumes there is such a thing as a clock.

00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:48.080
- Is this like a theoretical clock or is this actually like a real clock?

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:52.688
- Yeah, so, okay. So one of the things that I am quite happy with having done is,

00:42:52.688 --> 00:42:56.360
so this model is due to Page and Woodhurst.

00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:59.240
- Yes. This is an old model by the way, right?

00:42:59.240 --> 00:43:01.540
This is like many decades ago. I think in the '70s.

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:03.240
- I think in the '80s. - Oh, '80s, '80s.

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:07.740
Okay, yeah. - And a very nice model. One of the assumptions that went into it is,

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:10.500
as you're writing the point out, it's kind of strange is that there's no interactions

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:13.520
between the clock and the rest of the universe.

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:16.910
So it kind of feels, if you again, 'cause now, I was saying,

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:19.820
well, you can look at your watch, but this is not a clock you can look at.

00:43:19.820 --> 00:43:23.980
- Yes. - And since then, so I think what has happened is that initially,

00:43:23.980 --> 00:43:27.640
people kind of were very critical of this model.

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:30.520
It went out of vogue and now it's in vogue again.

00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:35.310
And people worked on it a lot. One of the things they've done is shown that even when

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:40.480
there's interactions, you can still get these, let's call them Page-Woodhurst states.

00:43:40.480 --> 00:43:44.547
So you can still get timeless descriptions, even when there are certain kinds of interactions,

00:43:44.547 --> 00:43:47.200
not general kinds of interactions, but certain kinds of interactions between the clock

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:54.520
and the rest of the universe. And building on that, Simona Rijovec and I showed that,

00:43:54.520 --> 00:43:58.240
in particular, you can measure the clock without disturbing the model.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:01.740
So you still have the timeless view emerging from it.

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:08.267
So you still have different universes being different times and the clock giving you

00:44:08.267 --> 00:44:13.320
the equations of motion that were used to all the good stuff.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:15.120
But the only thing that's different is that, you can actually observe it.

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:19.380
So you can say, okay, well, I can see, for example, - It won't affect the system.

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:22.707
- So you, yeah, you won't disturb it in such a way that the clock no longer functions

00:44:22.707 --> 00:44:26.907
as a clock. - Okay. - I think that's the technical way of putting it or the technically

00:44:26.907 --> 00:44:30.080
correct way of putting it anyway. So that means that, for example,

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:35.720
according to this extended model, your wristwatch is really a clock in this sense.

00:44:35.720 --> 00:44:39.620
So you can look at it and you provide it that you don't destroy it.

00:44:39.620 --> 00:44:43.860
You don't do certain things to it that completely ruin it in its function as a clock.

00:44:43.860 --> 00:44:46.640
You can observe it and measure the time.

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:50.600
Yeah, that's one of the ways in which the model has been advanced.

00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:56.280
So it's becoming ever more physical, I would say, and we have an ever better understanding

00:44:56.280 --> 00:45:00.591
of how these clocks should work. So I think it's fair to say that your wristwatch counts

00:45:00.591 --> 00:45:07.438
as a clock in this sense. - So time isn't just like a sequence of these universes or

00:45:07.438 --> 00:45:10.140
like, how do we best like recap it?

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:15.420
Like if I had to explain this to my mom or something, who is not a physicist,

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:21.040
what is the best way to like phrase this, to think about time then from this like quantum

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:25.300
mechanical perspective? - I think the best way to view it is that in this view,

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:32.448
each moment of time is equally real. And so the past exists,

00:45:32.448 --> 00:45:35.320
the future exists, they're not coming into existence.

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:39.540
I think that the typical view is they come into existence and then they cease to exist instantly.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:43.420
And there's this species present, which is constantly moving forward.

00:45:43.420 --> 00:45:48.080
That's not what's happening according to this understanding.

00:45:48.080 --> 00:45:54.400
What's happening instead is you're finding yourself in these different universes,

00:45:54.400 --> 00:45:58.380
but they already, like the universes are all there.

00:45:58.380 --> 00:46:02.440
If we were to say, what was the state of the universe like five minutes ago?

00:46:02.440 --> 00:46:08.554
Well, then there is a world that exists, which is the state of the universe five minutes

00:46:08.554 --> 00:46:13.200
ago, where we were talking. And in some sense, we're still talking.

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:19.233
We're still, us of five minutes ago are still having that conversation five minutes ago,

00:46:19.233 --> 00:46:24.420
if that makes sense. - So it's basically all happening at the same time.

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:28.717
- Yes, so yeah, it's hard to find a good terminology for this 'cause at the same time,

00:46:28.717 --> 00:46:34.500
it's kind of a misnomer, but it's kind of like maybe an analogy that is helpful is,

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:41.260
so perhaps this will go on YouTube, perhaps this will go on a podcasting app.

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:44.600
When you're listening to it, you're hearing, you're hearing a particular moment from

00:46:44.600 --> 00:46:48.320
the interview, but the moments are already all there.

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:52.700
Like you can go to any part in the interview and the recording is already completely,

00:46:52.700 --> 00:46:57.140
it's already been recorded. It's not like our voices are coming in and out of existence.

00:46:57.140 --> 00:47:04.600
They are already completely present in the recording of the conversation or like with books.

00:47:04.600 --> 00:47:07.860
I mean, a story already exists before you read it.

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:12.760
You're just on page 10 and you're gonna go to page 11, but page 10 still exists.

00:47:12.760 --> 00:47:15.720
And you can reread it and the same things will happen again.

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:18.680
And that's kind of the picture of the universe that this gives you.

00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:20.560
- What is happening in the future already exists.

00:47:20.560 --> 00:47:22.540
What is happening in the past already exists.

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:30.520
So how do we then think of free will and your free agency in that context?

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:33.420
- The future might already exist, but we can't predict it.

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:37.720
We can't predict from the current state of the universe what the future will entail.

00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:41.980
- And I'm guessing you can affect in which branch you end up in.

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:48.500
Is that right? - In some sense. If you have to explain why the story unfolds as it does,

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:52.420
you have to account for people's motives, their actions, their ideas.

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:57.262
And even though the book is already there in full, the reason why,

00:47:57.262 --> 00:48:02.632
for example, a hero defeats a villain might still be because of the various things he

00:48:02.632 --> 00:48:08.700
did throughout the book. So his actions had consequences, even in the deterministic universe.

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:12.320
If every time you watch the movie, it had a completely different outcome,

00:48:12.320 --> 00:48:21.072
that would not really be congruous or something with this idea that his actions have

00:48:21.072 --> 00:48:24.080
results, that he's doing something in the world.

00:48:24.080 --> 00:48:29.696
So we can have explanations of people and stories and why their actions were good and

00:48:29.696 --> 00:48:32.220
why they affected the world in certain ways.

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:36.600
And I think the same is true for us, even though we live in a deterministic universe.

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:40.040
- Actions have consequences and they do still need to be logical.

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:44.252
If I jump from the fifth floor, to the grounds, I will get hurt,

00:48:44.252 --> 00:48:46.740
right? There's no universe where I won't get hurt.

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:52.053
Like I'm very few. - Yeah, it's a bit like, okay, if we think that free will doesn't

00:48:52.053 --> 00:48:56.020
exist and we just lie down on the couch forever, well then we won't do anything.

00:48:56.020 --> 00:49:01.880
So, you know, lying down on the couch had results, predictable results,

00:49:01.880 --> 00:49:08.140
maybe even. And whereas undertaking certain actions will have different results.

00:49:08.140 --> 00:49:11.880
And we know that in advance, we have a choice to sit on the couch or not to sit on the couch.

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:17.960
And our actions still have consequences, even if it's determined.

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:21.680
I mean, kind of because it's deterministic, our actions have consequences.

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:27.600
I mean, I don't have a full theory of free will, but this is roughly how I think about it.

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:30.540
- Yeah, and it's an active area of research, right?

00:49:30.540 --> 00:49:34.880
- Yeah, it's not something I work on. But if you were to ask me as a physicist,

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:36.380
how do you think you can incorporate it with that?

00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:38.860
It would be something like this. - Yes, that makes sense.

00:49:38.860 --> 00:49:40.700
Perfect, well, thank you so much for your time.

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:43.280
This was a pleasure. And let's do this again soon.

00:49:43.280 --> 00:49:44.840
Sounds good. Thanks for having me. Sounds great.

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:47.200
Thank you.
